# UI work: the central render harness

There is one render harness for the whole app: `scripts/ui-look/look.ts`. It runs the real Electron
renderer, built as the perf app (`dist/perf.html`), in a hidden window against the perf boot's fake
backend (`src/perf/app/boot.ts`, `backend.ts`, `lenientClient.ts`, `src/test/fakeSupabase.ts`), and
`window.__blueplmLook` (`src/perf/app/look.ts`) puts the app into named scenes. Steps on the command
line click, type, evaluate and screenshot, so a screen or a whole flow is designed and checked by
reading its PNGs. Set by the user on 2026-11-04: build on this harness for this sort of development.

- Use it for any UI design or UX change, and to walk a multi-step flow end to end (for example a
  review from package to approval, with comments and a kickback). Render every state the change
  touches: empty, loading, error, populated, narrow and wide, light and dark where it matters.
- Never open the live app, a dev server or a browser to look at a screen (the user's global
  no-visual-verification rule). This harness is the one sanctioned exception: it never touches the
  user's running app, vault or user data.
- Extend the harness; never build a second one. New screens get a scene in `src/perf/app/look.ts`
  (or a per-area module it imports when the file grows), fixture data beside the existing
  `look*Fixtures.ts` files, and server behaviour as RPCs or rows on the same `FakeSupabase`
  (`defineRpc`, `seed`). Scenes drive the store through the same actions the UI calls.
- A stateful flow (several users, several steps) keeps its state in the fake backend, and scenes
  switch the acting user through the look control, so the same run can go author, reviewer, author.
- Name scenes `<area>-<state>` (`inspection-reports`, `review-detail-kickback`), list them with
  `--list`, and document new areas with their command sequence in `scripts/render-perf/README.md`.
- Scenes for an area live in `src/perf/app/lookScenes/<area>.ts` and start from a reset, so each
  is deterministic and scenes can run one after another. A whole flow is a steps file in
  `scripts/ui-look/flows/` (`--steps <file>`), for example `review-full.steps`.
- Several agents share the checkout: always build and run from your own folder with
  `--dist .ui-look/<name>` (gitignored), so builds never clobber each other. Build once with
  `--build`, then run without it; rebuild after changing renderer code, or the shots show old code.
  If another agent's half-finished edit breaks the build, render from a `git archive HEAD` copy
  plus your own files rather than waiting or editing theirs.
- `--as <user>` switches the acting user (store user, RPC actor and session together);
  `--theme light|dark`; `--rclick`, `--ctrlclick`, `--dblclick`, `--drag x1,y1,x2,y2`. In Git Bash
  set `MSYS_NO_PATHCONV=1` so selectors and paths are not rewritten.
- Keep it light: one hidden Electron per agent at a time, and never alongside a local test or tsc
  run.
- Screenshots go to a scratch folder, never into the repository.
- A screen the harness cannot reach yet is a harness gap: add the scene. Only when that is not
  possible, ask the user to look, saying exactly what to check.

```
npx tsx scripts/ui-look/look.ts --dist .ui-look/<you> --build --list
npx tsx scripts/ui-look/look.ts --dist .ui-look/<you> --scene <name> --shot <name> --out <scratch dir>
npx tsx scripts/ui-look/look.ts --dist .ui-look/<you> --as sam --scene <name> --click "text=Approve" --shot after --out <dir>
npx tsx scripts/ui-look/look.ts --dist .ui-look/<you> --theme dark --steps scripts/ui-look/flows/review-full.steps --out <dir>
```
